'''Artificial life''' (ALife) is an interdisciplinary field that studies life-like organization, adaptation and evolution through constructed systems. These may be computer simulations, robots or engineered chemical and biological systems.

== Historical scope ==
Christopher Langton's 1987 workshop and 1989 proceedings helped establish artificial life as a research field. Its constructive approach complements biological observation: researchers build systems with specified rules to test whether properties such as replication, adaptation or collective organization can arise. Software-based, hardware-based and biochemical approaches are often called soft, hard and wet artificial life, respectively; boundaries can overlap.

== Experimental mechanisms ==
A digital evolutionary model specifies heritable representations, reproduction, variation and competition for limited resources. A robot experiment specifies sensors, actuators, control rules and available energy. A biochemical model specifies reaction and transport kinetics and conditions for maintenance. Test mechanisms by perturbing or ablating them and comparing appropriate controls. A simulation of replication is not the same experimental object as a physically replicating chemical system.

== Open-endedness and definitions ==
Increasing task fitness alone does not imply open-ended evolution. Evaluate diversity, novelty, persistence and ecological interaction over specified time horizons, distinguishing new structures from changes in labels or trivial combinatorial variants. A program may rely on a host scheduler and external electricity, while a biochemical system relies on environmental nutrients; compare the actual maintenance mechanisms, not superficial dependence. Autopoiesis, reproduction and evolution capture different properties, and no single definition of life is universally accepted. Artificial life is distinct from conventional AI, which primarily targets computational capabilities rather than the organization of life, although their methods intersect.

